<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">probably the late 1860s (sometime after 1865)</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Swing Low, Sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">Chariot</a>&quot; was probably composed sometime after 1865 by Wallace Willis and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a> Willis, Choctaw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">Freedmen</a> living in the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>, near Hugo in what is now Choctaw County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Wallace Willis may have been inspired by the sight of the Red River, by which he worked, which reminded him of the Jordan River and of the Prophet Elijah being taken up to heaven in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> (2 Kings 2:11). Alexander Reid, a minister at the Old Spencer Academy, a Choctaw boarding school, heard Willis sing this song and Steal Away, transcribed their words and melodies and sent them to the Jubilee Singers of Fisk University in Nashville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, who made them known on a tour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The earliest known recording was made in 1894 by the Standard Quartette.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The song uses the theme of death to speak of the glory that Christians believe awaits them in heaven, beyond the earthly world of suffering. It was traditionally sung as call and response: a leader sings each line and the congregation answers Coming for to carry me home. Commonly sung without accompaniment, it allowed sudden changes of tempo, pitch and emphasis, and its melody is pentatonic. As with other folk songs there is no fixed text. The historian Charshee Charlotte Lawrence McIntyre argues that spirituals carried coded double meanings, here the Jordan as the first step to freedom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and home as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, though no such reading can be proved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Fisk University Jubilee Quartet's recording for Victor on 1 December 1909 became the first gospel song to sell a million copies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a>, Bing Crosby, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-armstrong">Louis Armstrong</a> and Sam Cooke were among those who recorded it later. In 1939 the Reich Music Examination Office of Nazi Germany listed it as undesired and harmful. It returned during the civil rights struggle and the folk revival of the 1960s, and Joan Baez sang it at Woodstock in 1969. In 2002 the Library of Congress added the Fisk Jubilee Singers' recording to the National Recording Registry, and in 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> made it the official state gospel song.</p><p>English rugby supporters have sung it for decades; the first documented singing at an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> international came at Twickenham against Ireland in 1988. In 2020 the Rugby Football Union said it was reviewing the fans' use of the song.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swing Low, Sweet Chariot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Steal Away&quot; is counted, with &quot;<b>Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</b>&quot;, &quot;Wade in the Water&quot; and &quot;The Gospel Train&quot;, among the spirituals said to carry hidden codes: besides their faith in God, messages telling enslaved people to run away, on their own or by the Underground Railroad.</blockquote><a href="/songs/steal-away">Steal Away</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</b>&quot; was probably composed sometime after 1865 by Wallace Willis and his daughter Minerva Willis, Choctaw Freedmen living in the old Indian Territory, near Hugo in what is now Choctaw County, Oklahoma.</blockquote><a href="/songs/swing-low-sweet-chariot">Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</a></li></ul></section>
